# Boot scripts that the install package carries and that no image installs into
# /etc/init.d.
#
# kvmapp/system/init.d is the application's own reference copy. /etc/init.d is
# what boots. A script that lands in only one of them does not run, and the
# gap is silent: the package looks right, the image looks right, and the
# behaviour is the base's.
#
# Every script in kvmapp/system/init.d must be named either by
# tools/abslots/manifest/root.manifest or by this file.
# tools/abslots/check-init-install.sh enforces that, and it checks the two
# reasons below differently.
#
#   same-as-base            The base rootfs carries this script and the fork
#                           does not change it, so installing our copy would
#                           write the same bytes. The check compares them. The
#                           moment the fork edits one of these the reason stops
#                           being true, the check fails, and the script has to
#                           move to root.manifest.
#
#   not-installed-by-policy The fork keeps this out of /etc/init.d on purpose.
#                           The check compares nothing, because the point is
#                           that the script must not run at boot.
#
# Format: <script> <reason> [note]

# The server owns this one. It writes /etc/init.d/S00cpufreq when Settings >
# Device > Advanced selects a non-stock clock and removes it again on stock, so
# an image must not carry it. See server/service/vm/cpufreq.go.
S00cpufreq       not-installed-by-policy  the server installs and removes it

# Daemons the image deliberately never starts. install.sh installed the whole
# directory once, and an update then started six daemons that the same
# release's image does not start.
S03usbhid        not-installed-by-policy  the gadget is built by S03usbdev
S50ssdpd         not-installed-by-policy  no discovery daemon in the fork's boot
S96picoclaw      not-installed-by-policy  started on demand, not at boot
S98tailscaled    not-installed-by-policy  started on demand, not at boot

# Carried for reference, identical to the base. The base copy is the one that
# runs, and it is the same file.
S15kvmhwd        same-as-base
S50avahi-daemon  same-as-base
S50sshd          same-as-base
S80dnsmasq       same-as-base
